// Lessons written from NHIE Volume 1, chapter 1 (Electrical System). "p" is the book page range to read.
// teach lines: "# " heading, "- " bullet, "! " memory tip, anything else is a paragraph.
// cards: [front, back]. qs: [question, [correct, wrong, wrong, wrong], explanation].
NHIE.lessons("electrical", [
{ t: "The words of the electrical system", p: "1–4",
  goal: "Name each part of the system the way the book and the exam name it.",
  teach: [
    "The exam uses exact words. If you know the words, half of the electrical questions become easy. Learn them in the order the electricity travels.",
    "# From the street to the house",
    "- Service drop: the utility wires that come to the house overhead. Service lateral: the same wires, but underground.",
    "- Service point: where the utility's wires end and the house's wires begin. With a service drop this is usually at the drip loops.",
    "- Service entrance conductors: the wires from the service point to the service equipment.",
    "- Service equipment: the breakers, switches or fuses that shut off all power to the house. People call it the main disconnect.",
    "# Inside the house",
    "- Panelboard (panel, load center): the equipment that holds the breakers or fuses. It sits inside an enclosure (the metal box).",
    "- Dead front cover: the inner cover you remove to see the wiring. It is behind the door.",
    "- Bus: the heavy metal bar the breakers attach to. Terminal bars hold the neutral and ground wires. Lug: a connection point where a wire is clamped.",
    "- Subpanel: a panel fed from another panel. Feeder conductors: the wires that feed it.",
    "- Branch circuit: wires that start at one breaker or fuse and serve outlets. An outlet is any place power is used: a receptacle, a light, a water heater connection.",
    "- Cable is two or more conductors in a sheath (NM cable is often called Romex, armored cable is BX). A raceway is a tube such as conduit that holds wires.",
    "# The three kinds of wire",
    "- Ungrounded conductor = the hot wire. Grounded conductor = the neutral wire. Do not mix up grounded and grounding.",
    "- Equipment grounding conductor (EGC) = the bare or green wire. It has been in most house wiring since about 1960.",
    "- Grounding electrode = metal touching the earth (a rod, a water pipe, steel in the footing). Grounding electrode conductor (GEC) = the wire that goes to it.",
    "! Grounded = neutral. Grounding = the bare or green safety wire. One letter changes the answer."
  ],
  cards: [
    ["Service drop vs service lateral", "Drop is overhead. Lateral is underground."],
    ["Service point", "Where the utility's wires end and the house service entrance conductors begin; usually at the drip loops for a service drop."],
    ["Service equipment", "The breakers, switches or fuses that shut off all electricity to the house (main disconnect)."],
    ["Grounded conductor", "The neutral wire. It is intentionally connected to the earth."],
    ["EGC", "Equipment grounding conductor: the bare or green wire that gives fault current a path back. Common since about 1960."],
    ["GEC", "Grounding electrode conductor: the wire between the electrical system and the grounding electrode."],
    ["Outlet", "Any place where current is taken for use: a receptacle, a light fixture, an appliance connection."],
    ["Dead front cover", "The panel cover that is removed to reach the energized parts inside the enclosure."]
  ],
  qs: [
    ["Which term means the neutral wire?", ["Grounded conductor", "Grounding conductor", "Ungrounded conductor", "Grounding electrode conductor"], "Grounded = neutral. Ungrounded = hot. Grounding = the bare or green equipment wire."],
    ["Underground utility wires from the transformer to the house are called the:", ["Service lateral", "Service drop", "Feeder", "Branch circuit"], "Lateral is underground; drop is overhead."],
    ["Wires that run from the service equipment to a subpanel are:", ["Feeder conductors", "Branch circuit conductors", "Service entrance conductors", "Grounding electrode conductors"], "Feeders supply another panel. Branch circuits start at a breaker and serve outlets."],
    ["A hard-wired connection for an electric water heater is an example of:", ["An outlet", "A receptacle", "A raceway", "A lug"], "An outlet is any point where current is taken for use. A receptacle is one kind of outlet, made to accept a plug."]
  ] },

{ t: "How electricity works", p: "4–7",
  goal: "Explain volts, amps, watts and ohms, and use the two formulas.",
  teach: [
    "Electricity leaves a source, travels on the hot wire, does work at a load (a lamp, a range), and returns to the source on the neutral wire. Source, load, source: it is always a loop.",
    "# Four measurements",
    "- Voltage (volts, E): the pressure that pushes electricity. Think of water pressure.",
    "- Current (amps, I): how much electricity is flowing. Think of gallons per minute.",
    "- Power (watts, P): the work being done.",
    "- Resistance (ohms, R): how much a material holds the flow back. Low resistance = conductor. High resistance = insulator.",
    "# Two formulas",
    "- Watt's Law: Power = Volts x Amps. A 15 amp, 120 volt circuit has 1,800 watts available.",
    "- Ohm's Law: Amps = Volts / Ohms. A 100 watt bulb at 120 volts draws 0.83 amps (100 / 120).",
    "- Long wires and small wires have more resistance, so the voltage at the far end drops.",
    "# What comes into a house",
    "- Alternating current (AC) at 60 cycles per second. Direct current (DC) flows one way and comes from batteries.",
    "- Almost every house has single phase 240/120 volt service: two hot wires and one neutral. Hot to hot = 240 volts. Hot to neutral = 120 volts. Three phase is for commercial buildings.",
    "# Old work and new rules",
    "- Rule 1: existing work does not have to be updated if it met the rules when installed and is working safely.",
    "- Rule 2: new, changed or extended work should meet the rules in force when the work is done, permit or no permit.",
    "- So the inspector uses judgment on old work, and usually reports new or changed work that does not meet current rules. It is good to recommend asking whether permits were obtained.",
    "! P = E x I. Cover the one you want: watts = volts x amps, amps = watts / volts."
  ],
  cards: [
    ["Voltage", "Electrical pressure, measured in volts (E). Like water pressure."],
    ["Current", "The amount of electricity flowing, measured in amps (I). Like gallons per minute."],
    ["Watt's Law", "Power (watts) = Volts x Amps."],
    ["Ohm's Law", "Amps = Volts / Ohms (Resistance = Volts / Amps)."],
    ["Watts available on a 15 amp, 120 volt circuit", "1,800 watts (120 x 15)."],
    ["Normal house service", "Single phase, 240/120 volts: two hot conductors and one grounded (neutral) conductor."],
    ["Frequency of AC power in North America", "60 cycles per second."],
    ["Does existing work have to meet today's rules?", "No, if it met the rules when installed and is functioning safely. New, modified or extended work should."]
  ],
  qs: [
    ["A 20 amp, 120 volt circuit can supply how many watts?", ["2,400", "1,800", "140", "6"], "Watts = volts x amps = 120 x 20 = 2,400."],
    ["A 1,200 watt heater on a 120 volt circuit draws:", ["10 amps", "0.1 amps", "144 amps", "1,320 amps"], "Amps = watts / volts = 1,200 / 120 = 10."],
    ["What is the voltage between the two hot conductors of a normal house service?", ["240 volts", "120 volts", "0 volts", "480 volts"], "Hot to hot is 240. Hot to neutral is 120."],
    ["An owner added a basement bathroom last year without GFCI protection. The house was built in 1960. How should the inspector treat it?", ["Report it: new work should meet the rules in force when the work was done", "Ignore it: the house is old", "Report the whole house as non-compliant", "Report it only if a permit was issued"], "Old work may stay as it was if safe. New, modified or extended work should comply with current rules, with or without a permit."],
    ["What happens to voltage at the end of a very long, small wire carrying a steady load?", ["It drops, because resistance is higher", "It rises", "It stays exactly the same", "It changes to DC"], "Longer and thinner wires have more resistance, so more voltage is lost along the way."]
  ] },

{ t: "Grounding and bonding: what each one does", p: "7–10",
  goal: "Explain why bonding, not the ground rod, is what trips the breaker and saves a person.",
  teach: [
    "This is the idea the exam tests most in electrical. People mix the two up. Neither one is used in normal daily operation; both are safety systems.",
    "# Grounding: mostly protects equipment",
    "- The grounding system connects the house electrical system to the earth.",
    "- Its main job is voltage surges: lightning near the transformer, a high voltage line falling on the service drop, switching surges. It gives that energy a path to earth. It acts like a surge suppressor (it is not a real one).",
    "# Bonding: mostly protects people",
    "- Bonding joins metal that is not supposed to carry current (water pipes, gas pipes, metal boxes, appliance cases) into a permanent, low resistance path back to the source.",
    "- A ground fault is when a hot wire touches that metal. If the metal is bonded, a large current flows at once and the breaker trips. That is called clearing the fault.",
    "- If the metal is not bonded, no current flows, the breaker does not trip, and the metal stays live until a person touches it and becomes the path.",
    "# Why the path must be low resistance",
    "- Book example: a loose or painted clamp adds 10 ohms. 120 volts / 10 ohms = 12 amps. Twelve amps will not trip a 15 amp breaker, but it can kill.",
    "# Two myths the exam likes",
    "- Myth: electricity wants to go to the ground. Truth: it wants to return to its source, the utility transformer.",
    "- Myth: electricity takes the path of least resistance. Truth: it takes all available paths; each path carries current according to its resistance.",
    "- The equipment grounding conductor (EGC) in a cable is really a bonding wire. It is not part of the grounding electrode system.",
    "! Grounding = surges and equipment. Bonding = faults and people. The ground rod does not trip the breaker."
  ],
  cards: [
    ["Main purpose of the grounding system", "To give voltage surges (lightning, line surges) a safe path to earth. It mostly protects equipment."],
    ["Main purpose of bonding", "To give ground fault current a low resistance path back to the source so the breaker or fuse opens. It mostly protects people."],
    ["Ground fault", "Electricity flowing in metal that is not normally energized, usually because a hot wire touched it."],
    ["Why must a bonding path be low resistance?", "With high resistance, too little current flows to trip the breaker, and the metal stays energized."],
    ["Where does electricity want to return?", "To its source (the utility transformer), not to the earth."],
    ["Which path does electricity take?", "All available paths. More current flows on the lower resistance paths."],
    ["The EGC is really a ...", "Bonding wire. It is not part of the grounding electrode system."]
  ],
  qs: [
    ["A hot wire touches a metal water pipe that is properly bonded. What should happen?", ["A large current flows and the breaker trips", "Nothing, because the pipe is grounded", "The pipe stays energized until someone touches it", "The voltage in the house doubles"], "Bonding gives the fault a low resistance path to the source. Current rises past the breaker rating and the breaker clears the fault."],
    ["A bonding clamp is fastened over paint and has 10 ohms of resistance. On a 120 volt fault, how much current flows, and what is the result?", ["12 amps; a 15 amp breaker will not trip", "1,200 amps; the breaker trips at once", "0 amps; there is no hazard", "120 amps; the main breaker trips"], "120 / 10 = 12 amps. That is under 15 amps so the breaker stays on, but it is enough to kill."],
    ["Which statement about the grounding system of a house is correct?", ["It helps send voltage surges safely to earth", "It is what trips a breaker during a ground fault", "It carries current in normal operation", "It replaces the need for bonding"], "Grounding has little effect on clearing ground faults. Bonding does that work."],
    ["Electricity returning to its source will:", ["Take all available paths, more on the lower resistance ones", "Take only the path of least resistance", "Always go into the earth", "Stop at the neutral bar"], "This is why a person touching energized metal still gets some current even when a bonded path exists, but only until the breaker trips."]
  ] },

{ t: "Grounding electrodes and bonding rules", p: "10–12 and 25–26",
  goal: "Know the electrode sizes, the wire sizes, and the defects to report.",
  teach: [
    "# Grounding electrodes: 20, 8, 10",
    "- Concrete-encased (Ufer) electrode: at least 20 feet of #4 rebar or #4 bare copper in the concrete footing.",
    "- Rod or pipe: driven at least 8 feet into the ground.",
    "- Metal water service pipe (or metal well casing): in contact with the earth for at least 10 feet.",
    "- All electrodes that are present must be bonded together. In newer houses a water pipe cannot be the only electrode, and a single rod needs a second electrode.",
    "# Grounding electrode conductor (GEC) size, copper",
    "- #8 for services of 125 amps and smaller.",
    "- #6 for 150 amps and larger.",
    "- #4 for a Ufer electrode with a 200 amp or larger service.",
    "- In newer houses the connection to a water pipe electrode must be within 5 feet of where the pipe enters the house.",
    "# One connection only",
    "- The GEC joins the neutral at the service equipment (or just before it). After that point, neutrals and grounds must not be connected together again. A second connection makes normal neutral current flow on the bonding wires and on metal parts.",
    "# What gets bonded",
    "- If it is metal and it is or could be near electrical wires, bond it: metal water and gas pipes, conduit, equipment cabinets, metal framing, ducts. Coax for cable and satellite TV too.",
    "- Clamps must be on clean metal: no paint, rust or dirt.",
    "- A bonding jumper is needed around anything that breaks the metal path: a water meter, pressure regulator, water softener or filter, plastic fitting.",
    "- CSST gas tubing is bonded at the first metal gas pipe or fitting after the meter.",
    "# Report these",
    "- GEC loose, damaged, disconnected, too small, or spliced wrongly (only a listed compression connector or a weld is allowed; never solder).",
    "- Clamp missing, loose, or on a painted or corroded surface.",
    "- Metal conduit joined with plastic fittings, or loose conduit joints (the bonding path is broken).",
    "- Water pipe electrode connection more than 5 feet from where the pipe enters.",
    "- No jumper across the water meter or softener; CSST not bonded.",
    "! Ufer 20, rod 8, water pipe 10, connect within 5."
  ],
  cards: [
    ["Ufer (concrete-encased) electrode", "At least 20 feet of #4 rebar or #4 bare copper encased in the concrete footing."],
    ["Ground rod depth", "Driven at least 8 feet into the ground."],
    ["Water pipe as a grounding electrode", "Metal pipe in contact with the earth for at least 10 feet; connect within 5 feet of where it enters (newer houses)."],
    ["GEC size for 125 amp and smaller service", "#8 AWG copper."],
    ["GEC size for 150 amp and larger service", "#6 AWG copper (#4 for a Ufer electrode at 200 amps or more)."],
    ["How may a GEC be spliced?", "Only with a listed compression connector or by welding. Not soldered."],
    ["When is a bonding jumper needed on water piping?", "Around a water meter, pressure regulator, softener, filter or any removable or non-metal part."],
    ["Where is CSST bonded?", "At the first metal gas pipe or fitting after the gas meter."]
  ],
  qs: [
    ["A copper-coated ground rod must be driven at least how far into the ground?", ["8 feet", "4 feet", "10 feet", "20 feet"], "Rod or pipe: 8 feet. Water pipe: 10 feet of contact. Ufer: 20 feet."],
    ["A house has 200 amp service and a ground rod. What is the minimum copper GEC size?", ["#6 AWG", "#8 AWG", "#12 AWG", "#14 AWG"], "#8 is only for 125 amps and smaller. 150 amps and larger needs #6 (or #4 with a Ufer at 200 amps and up)."],
    ["A water softener was installed in a copper water line. What should the inspector look for?", ["A bonding jumper around the softener", "A ground rod at the softener", "A GFCI on the softener", "A second water meter"], "The softener interrupts the metal path. A jumper keeps the pipe on both sides bonded."],
    ["The grounding electrode conductor has been cut and soldered back together. This is:", ["A defect: only a listed compression connector or a weld is allowed", "Acceptable", "Acceptable if taped", "Required for aluminum wire"], "Solder can melt under fault current and open the connection."],
    ["The neutral bar and ground bar are bonded together in a subpanel in the garage of the same house. Why is this wrong?", ["Normal neutral current will flow on bonding wires and metal parts", "The breakers will trip too fast", "The voltage will be too low", "It is not wrong"], "Neutral and ground join only once, at the service equipment. Downstream panels keep them separate."]
  ] },

{ t: "Service size and required circuits", p: "12–14",
  goal: "Work out the service amperage and know which circuits a modern house needs.",
  teach: [
    "# Voltage and current",
    "- Normal service is 240 volt single phase. A 120 volt only service is usually a reportable deficiency. Nobody supplies 220 volts today.",
    "- The minimum for a modern single-family house is 100 amps. Less than 100 is a reportable deficiency, even when original. Each unit of a two-family house or townhouse may have 60 amps, but tell the client.",
    "- Newer houses usually have 150 or 200 amps. Large houses may have up to 400.",
    "# Finding the service amperage",
    "- You must report it. It is the lowest rated part of the chain: service entrance wires, service equipment, panelboard.",
    "- Book example: 2/0 copper wires (good for 200 amps) with 150 amp equipment and panel = 150 amp service.",
    "- Do not use the meter or the meter base to decide.",
    "- Danger case: a new larger panel and main breaker on the old, smaller service entrance wires. The wires can overheat. Report it.",
    "- If you cannot tell (for example several disconnects in several enclosures), say so and recommend an electrician.",
    "# Circuits a modern house needs",
    "- Two 20 amp, 120 volt circuits for kitchen countertop receptacles (they may also serve the dining and breakfast room receptacles and the refrigerator).",
    "- One 20 amp, 120 volt circuit for laundry receptacles only.",
    "- One 20 amp, 120 volt circuit for bathroom receptacles only.",
    "- A circuit that serves only the furnace or air handler.",
    "- Most 240 volt appliances have their own dedicated circuit. Older houses without these circuits are not automatically deficient.",
    "# Multiwire branch circuits",
    "- Two hot wires share one neutral: 240 volts between the hots, 120 volts from each hot to neutral.",
    "- Examples: split-wired kitchen receptacles, dryer and range circuits. Water heaters and condensers usually have no neutral, so they are not multiwire.",
    "- The two breakers need an approved handle tie so both hots turn off together.",
    "- A service load calculation is done by an electrician. It is out of scope for the inspector.",
    "! The service is only as big as its smallest part."
  ],
  cards: [
    ["Minimum service for a modern single-family house", "100 amps. Smaller is a reportable deficiency."],
    ["How is service amperage determined?", "By the lowest rated component among the service entrance conductors, service equipment and panelboard. Not by the meter."],
    ["New 200 amp panel on old 100 amp service wires", "A dangerous defect: the wires can carry more current than they can handle. The service is still 100 amps."],
    ["Required kitchen countertop circuits", "Two 20 amp, 120 volt circuits."],
    ["Multiwire branch circuit", "Two hot conductors sharing one neutral. Needs a handle tie on the breakers."],
    ["Is a 120 volt only service acceptable?", "Usually a reportable deficiency."],
    ["Who does a service load calculation?", "A qualified electrician. It is outside the home inspection."]
  ],
  qs: [
    ["Service entrance conductors are rated 200 amps, the main breaker is 150 amps and the panel is rated 200 amps. What is the service amperage?", ["150 amps", "200 amps", "350 amps", "100 amps"], "The lowest rated component sets the service size."],
    ["A 1950s house has its original 60 amp service. How is this reported?", ["As a deficiency: under the 100 amp minimum", "Acceptable because it is original", "Not reported", "Acceptable if fuses are used"], "Less than 100 amps is a reportable deficiency for a single-family house even if original."],
    ["There are three service disconnects in three enclosures and the inspector cannot tell the service amperage. What is correct?", ["Report that it could not be determined and recommend an electrician", "Report the size of the meter", "Add up all the breakers", "Guess 100 amps"], "It is acceptable to say it could not be determined, with a recommendation for evaluation."],
    ["Two breakers serve a split-wired kitchen receptacle and share a neutral. What should connect the breaker handles?", ["An approved handle tie", "A nail", "Nothing", "A piece of wire"], "Both hot legs of a multiwire circuit must disconnect together."],
    ["Which of these is NOT a required dedicated circuit in a modern house?", ["A 20 amp circuit for bedroom receptacles only", "A 20 amp circuit for laundry receptacles", "A 20 amp circuit for bathroom receptacles", "Two 20 amp circuits for kitchen counters"], "Kitchen (two), laundry, bathroom and furnace circuits are required. Bedrooms have no special dedicated circuit."]
  ] },

{ t: "Service drop and service lateral", p: "15–19 and 23–25",
  goal: "Know every clearance number for overhead service wires.",
  teach: [
    "# The parts",
    "- A service drop is overhead, common on older and rural houses. A service lateral is underground and less likely to be damaged.",
    "- The drop attaches to the house wall or fascia, or to a service mast (a pipe above the roof). Wires enter the mast through the service head (gooseneck).",
    "- Drip loops: the wires dip below the service head so water drips off and does not run into the meter or panel. The service head points down.",
    "- Water in equipment causes corrosion, corrosion adds resistance, resistance makes heat, heat starts fires.",
    "# Clearance above the roof",
    "- Roof slope less than 4/12: 8 feet.",
    "- Roof slope 4/12 or steeper: 3 feet.",
    "- Exception, the one seen most often: 18 inches, when less than 6 feet of wire is over the roof and it passes over no more than 4 feet of roof.",
    "# Clearance above the ground",
    "- 10 feet above the ground or a walkway.",
    "- 12 feet above a residential driveway.",
    "- 18 feet above a public street or where trucks drive.",
    "- 22 1/2 feet above the water of a swimming pool, out to 10 feet from the pool edge.",
    "# Clearance to openings",
    "- 3 feet from the sides and the sill (bottom) of a window that opens, a door, deck or balcony. No clearance is required above the opening. The rule is for open individual wires, not wires in conduit.",
    "- Measure to the lowest wire, usually the drip loops. A new porch roof or addition can turn a good clearance into a defect.",
    "# Report these",
    "- Wires in tree limbs; damaged insulation; a bare splice at the service point.",
    "- Poor drip loops; loose or bent mast; bad mast flashing; drop pulling off the house.",
    "- Any clearance that is too small.",
    "- Loose or unsealed meter base or enclosure (water gets in behind it).",
    "! Going up: 10 walk, 12 drive, 18 street, 22 1/2 pool. Roof: 8 flat, 3 steep, 18 inches at the edge."
  ],
  cards: [
    ["Service drop over a roof with slope under 4/12", "8 feet."],
    ["Service drop over a roof with slope 4/12 or more", "3 feet."],
    ["The 18 inch exception", "Allowed when less than 6 feet of wire is over the roof and it crosses no more than 4 feet of roof."],
    ["Service drop above a walkway or the ground", "10 feet."],
    ["Service drop above a residential driveway", "12 feet."],
    ["Service drop above a public street", "18 feet."],
    ["Service drop above a swimming pool", "22 1/2 feet above the water, extending 10 feet from the pool edge."],
    ["Service drop clearance to a window that opens", "3 feet from the sides and sill. None required above."],
    ["Purpose of drip loops", "Water drips off the wires before they enter the service head, so it does not run into the meter or panel."]
  ],
  qs: [
    ["Service drop wires pass 9 feet above a residential driveway. Is this acceptable?", ["No. 12 feet is required", "Yes. 8 feet is required", "No. 18 feet is required", "Yes, if the wires are insulated"], "10 feet over walkways, 12 over residential driveways, 18 over streets."],
    ["The drip loops are 2 feet above a roof with a 6/12 slope, well back from the eave. Is this acceptable?", ["No. 3 feet is required", "Yes. 18 inches is required everywhere", "No. 8 feet is required", "Yes. No clearance applies to steep roofs"], "Slope of 4/12 or more needs 3 feet. The 18 inch exception is only for a short crossing at the roof edge."],
    ["Open service conductors run 1 foot above the top of a window that opens. What should be reported?", ["Nothing; clearance is not required above an opening", "A defect; 3 feet is required above", "A defect; 10 feet is required", "A defect; windows may not be under wires"], "3 feet applies to the sides and sill only."],
    ["Service entrance wires go straight into the service head with no downward loop. What is the concern?", ["Water can follow the wires into the meter base or panel", "The voltage will be too high", "The meter will run backward", "Birds cannot land"], "Report inadequate drip loops."],
    ["Why is a service lateral considered more desirable than a service drop?", ["The conductors are less likely to be damaged", "It carries more voltage", "It needs no meter", "It needs no grounding"], "Underground wires are protected from trees, weather and contact."]
  ] },

{ t: "Service equipment, panels and working space", p: "19–23",
  goal: "Know the six-disconnect rule, the panel types and the 36-30-78 working space.",
  teach: [
    "# Service equipment",
    "- It must shut off all power to the house with no more than six breakers, fuse pulls or switches, and all of them in one location.",
    "- It must be labeled as the disconnect and be listed as service equipment.",
    "- Fuse blocks are no longer allowed as new service equipment, but old ones may stay if safe and working.",
    "# Kinds of enclosure",
    "- Meter-main: meter, service equipment and panel in one enclosure.",
    "- Main breaker panel: service equipment and panel together.",
    "- Main lug panel: no main breaker; fed by feeders from somewhere else. Usually wired as a subpanel.",
    "- Any panel downstream of the service equipment is a subpanel: neutrals kept separate from grounds.",
    "- Split-bus panel (older): no single main breaker. The upper section holds up to six 240 volt breakers; one of them feeds the lower section with the 15 and 20 amp circuits. Turning off the upper breakers satisfies the six-disconnect rule.",
    "- Manufactured home: the service equipment is usually on a post in the yard, so the panel inside is a subpanel.",
    "# The enclosure itself",
    "- It must be listed for where it is. Outdoors or in a damp place it needs a 1/4 inch air space behind it.",
    "- In a finished wall the gap around it can be no more than 1/8 inch.",
    "- Equipment not inside a listed enclosure is a defect.",
    "# Where panels may not be",
    "- Clothes closets, bathrooms, storage spaces, above stair steps. A stair landing is fine if the working space is there.",
    "# Working space",
    "- 36 inches deep, 30 inches wide, 78 inches high, measured from the floor. The door must open at least 90 degrees.",
    "- The space directly above and below the enclosure is for electrical parts only: no pipes or ducts.",
    "- The highest breaker handle may be no more than 79 inches above the floor. There is no minimum height.",
    "- Readily accessible means you can reach it without moving things or using a ladder. A panel behind a picture, shelf or workbench is not readily accessible. A locked panel is allowed if the key is available.",
    "! Six disconnects. 36 deep, 30 wide, 78 high. Handle no higher than 79."
  ],
  cards: [
    ["Maximum number of disconnects to shut off a house", "Six breakers, fuse pulls or switches, in one location."],
    ["Split-bus panel", "Older panel with no single main. Up to six 240 volt breakers on the upper bus; one feeds the lower bus."],
    ["Main lug panel", "A panel with no main breaker, fed by feeder conductors; usually configured as a subpanel."],
    ["Working space in front of a panel", "36 inches deep, 30 inches wide, 78 inches high. Door opens at least 90 degrees."],
    ["Highest allowed breaker handle", "79 inches above the floor."],
    ["Where panels are not allowed", "Clothes closets, bathrooms, storage spaces and above stair steps."],
    ["Air space behind an outdoor or damp-location enclosure", "At least 1/4 inch."],
    ["Readily accessible", "Reachable without climbing over or moving objects and without a portable ladder."]
  ],
  qs: [
    ["A panel has no main breaker. The upper section has six 240 volt breakers, and one is marked lighting main. What is this?", ["A split-bus panel; it can be acceptable", "A defective panel that must be replaced", "A subpanel", "A meter-main"], "Split-bus panels meet the six-disconnect rule with the upper breakers. They are old, so the inspector may still comment on age."],
    ["A panel is in a bedroom clothes closet. How is this reported?", ["As a defect: panels may not be in clothes closets", "As acceptable", "As acceptable if the door is removed", "As cosmetic"], "Clothing is fuel next to a possible source of sparks, and the working space is blocked."],
    ["A washing machine stands directly in front of the panel. Which requirement is not met?", ["36 inches of clear depth in front", "The 79 inch handle height", "The 1/4 inch air space", "The six-disconnect rule"], "Working space is 36 deep, 30 wide and 78 high."],
    ["The panel of a manufactured home is fed from a disconnect on a post outside. How should the neutrals and grounds be arranged in the panel?", ["Separated, because it is a subpanel", "Together on one bar", "Neither is needed", "Grounds on the hot bus"], "The service equipment is on the post. Everything downstream is a subpanel."],
    ["A water pipe runs directly above the panel, inside its footprint. Is this a concern?", ["Yes. The space above and below the enclosure is for electrical equipment only", "No", "Only if the pipe is plastic", "Only outdoors"], "A leak would run into the panel."]
  ] },

{ t: "Opening the panel safely; fuses and breakers", p: "28–31",
  goal: "Know when not to open a panel, and how fuses and breakers protect the wires.",
  teach: [
    "# Two dangers for the inspector",
    "- Electrocution: touching an energized part. The enclosure itself can be live if a wire or a pointed screw touches it.",
    "- Arc-flash: a spark jumps from an energized part to a grounded part, with light and heat that can burn skin and eyes.",
    "- Before opening, touch the cover with the back of your hand. Remove and replace the dead front cover slowly. Wear rubber soles and safety glasses. You are not required to put anything inside the enclosure.",
    "# You may decide not to open it",
    "- You do not have to do any procedure you believe is unsafe for you, other people or the property. Examples: a known problem brand, access blocked by belongings.",
    "- Then you must report that it was not done and why. It is good practice to recommend that it be done and to say defects could exist.",
    "# What overcurrent protection devices (OPDs) do",
    "- Purpose 1: stop the current when it is too high (overload). Too much current makes heat that damages wire and insulation and can start a fire.",
    "- Purpose 2: stop the current during a short circuit, when sparks can start a fire.",
    "- OPDs protect the wires and the house. They are not there to protect people from shock.",
    "# Fuses",
    "- The main OPD in houses through the 1950s. Fused panels are usually past their service life.",
    "- Edison base (Type T): every size has the same screw base, so a 30 amp fuse fits where a 15 amp belongs. That is overfusing. Report it, and report a penny in a fuse holder. Not allowed in new installations.",
    "- Type S: each amp rating has a different base, so the wrong size will not fit. Adapters are made for Edison holders; recommending them is good practice.",
    "- Cartridge: a cylinder, used for mains and for equipment disconnects.",
    "- Type W opens at once (not for motors). Type L and D have a time delay.",
    "- Colors: 15 amp blue, 20 amp orange or red, 30 amp green.",
    "# Circuit breakers",
    "- In houses since the 1930s; common since the 1960s.",
    "- Modern breakers are thermal-magnetic. Thermal (heat) trips on overload. Magnetic trips on a short circuit.",
    "- Half-height breakers put more circuits in a panel, but only where the panel label allows them, and only up to the number of breakers the label permits.",
    "! Thermal = overload = slow. Magnetic = short circuit = instant."
  ],
  cards: [
    ["Two safety risks when opening a panel", "Electrocution and arc-flash."],
    ["Why touch the cover with the back of the hand first?", "The enclosure can be energized by a loose wire or a screw through insulation. A shocked muscle pulls the back of the hand away."],
    ["If the inspector does not open a panel", "Report that it was not inspected and why; recommend that it be done; advise that defects could exist."],
    ["Two purposes of an OPD", "Stop overcurrent (overload) and stop short circuits, so wires do not overheat and start a fire."],
    ["Problem with Edison base fuses", "All ratings fit the same socket, so a larger fuse can replace a smaller one (overfusing)."],
    ["Type S fuse", "Each rating has a different base, so the wrong size cannot be installed."],
    ["Thermal-magnetic breaker", "Thermal part trips on overload (heat); magnetic part trips on a short circuit."],
    ["Fuse colors: 15, 20, 30 amps", "15 blue, 20 orange or red, 30 green."]
  ],
  qs: [
    ["Boxes are stacked in front of the panel and the inspector cannot open it safely. What is required?", ["Report that the interior was not inspected and the reason", "Move the boxes and open it", "Report the panel as satisfactory", "Leave it out of the report"], "Inspectors are not required to move belongings or do unsafe procedures, but the limitation must be reported."],
    ["What is the main purpose of a circuit breaker?", ["To stop current that could overheat the wires", "To protect people from shock", "To keep voltage steady", "To save energy"], "Shock protection for people is the job of bonding and GFCIs."],
    ["A 30 amp Edison base fuse is found on a circuit with 14 gauge wire. What is this called?", ["Overfusing; a fire hazard to report", "Proper protection", "A time-delay installation", "Bonding"], "The wire is good for 15 amps. A 30 amp fuse lets it overheat. Recommend evaluation by an electrician."],
    ["Which part of a thermal-magnetic breaker reacts to a short circuit?", ["The magnetic part", "The thermal part", "The handle tie", "The lug"], "Magnetic trip is instant for short circuits. Thermal trip responds to the heat of an overload."],
    ["Half-height (thin) breakers are installed in every slot of a panel. What should the inspector check?", ["Whether the panel label allows them and in which slots", "Nothing; they fit any panel", "That they are all the same color", "That they are on the neutral bar"], "Too many breakers, or half-height breakers where the label does not permit them, is a defect."]
  ] },

{ t: "GFCI and AFCI", p: "31–33",
  goal: "Tell the two devices apart and know where each is required.",
  teach: [
    "The names look alike, but they protect against different dangers. The exam asks this again and again.",
    "# GFCI: protects people from shock",
    "- It compares the current on the hot wire with the current on the neutral. If they differ by about 6 milliamps, current is leaking somewhere (a ground fault), and it trips.",
    "- Required for receptacles where water may be present: kitchen countertops, bathrooms, garages, laundry rooms, unfinished basements, unfinished detached buildings, crawl spaces, outdoors. Also the dishwasher circuit.",
    "- It can be a receptacle or a breaker. Receptacles are more common because they cost less.",
    "- It does not need the equipment grounding wire to work. So the approved way to put a 3-slot receptacle on an old 2-wire circuit is a GFCI labeled NO EQUIPMENT GROUND.",
    "- History: bathrooms, garages and outdoors in the 1970s; kitchen counters in the 1990s; laundry and bar sinks in the 2000s.",
    "- Testing is not required, but many inspectors do it. Makers recommend the TEST button on the device. Testing cuts power to everything downstream, so check the refrigerator and freezer are running before you leave.",
    "# AFCI: protects the house from fire",
    "- It detects arcing (sparking) from damaged wires or cords.",
    "- Now required for most 15 and 20 amp, 120 volt circuits. Main exceptions: bathrooms, laundry rooms, basements, crawl spaces, garages and outdoors.",
    "- Protection should be an AFCI breaker. AFCI receptacles are only for changed or extended circuits where a breaker is not practical.",
    "- Old type: branch/feeder (parallel arcs only), not allowed since the late 2000s. Current type: combination, which detects parallel and series arcs. Combination does not mean it includes a GFCI.",
    "- First required for bedroom circuits in the early 2000s. Rules differ by area, so it is hard to know when to report a missing AFCI.",
    "- Testing is not required; many inspectors do not test them in occupied houses.",
    "# Breaker age",
    "- Breakers last about 30 to 50 years. Some inspectors recommend replacing panels and breakers older than about 50 years.",
    "! GFCI: G for guarding people. AFCI: F for fire."
  ],
  cards: [
    ["What does a GFCI protect against?", "Electric shock to people during a ground fault."],
    ["GFCI trip level", "A difference of about 6 milliamps between the hot and neutral conductors."],
    ["What does an AFCI protect against?", "Fire in the structure caused by arcing faults."],
    ["Does a GFCI need an equipment grounding conductor?", "No. It may be used on a circuit without one, labeled NO EQUIPMENT GROUND."],
    ["Where is GFCI protection required?", "Kitchen counters, bathrooms, garages, laundry rooms, unfinished basements, unfinished detached buildings, crawl spaces, outdoors, and the dishwasher circuit."],
    ["Combination AFCI", "Detects both parallel and series arc faults. It is not a GFCI."],
    ["Recommended way to test a GFCI", "Press the TEST button on the device."],
    ["Service life of a circuit breaker", "About 30 to 50 years."]
  ],
  qs: [
    ["A 1950s house has 2-wire circuits with no equipment ground. What is an approved way to install a 3-slot receptacle?", ["Install a GFCI receptacle labeled NO EQUIPMENT GROUND", "Connect the ground screw to the neutral screw", "Install a 3-slot receptacle and leave the ground open", "Run a wire to the nearest water faucet"], "A GFCI works without the EGC. A jumper to neutral is a bootleg ground and a shock hazard."],
    ["Which device is intended to protect the building from fire caused by damaged wiring?", ["AFCI", "GFCI", "Handle tie", "Type S fuse"], "AFCI = arc fault = fire. GFCI = ground fault = shock."],
    ["After testing GFCIs in a kitchen and garage, what should the inspector do before leaving?", ["Confirm the refrigerator and freezer are running", "Leave them tripped as proof of testing", "Remove the receptacles", "Nothing"], "Tripping a GFCI cuts power to downstream receptacles."],
    ["Which location is an exception where AFCI protection is generally not required?", ["Bathroom", "Bedroom", "Living room", "Hallway"], "Exceptions include bathrooms, laundry rooms, basements, crawl spaces, garages and outdoors."],
    ["A GFCI trips when it senses:", ["About 6 milliamps of difference between hot and neutral current", "15 amps of current", "A voltage of 240", "An arc in a cord"], "A breaker reacts to amps of overload; a GFCI reacts to thousandths of an amp of leakage."]
  ] },

{ t: "Wire size, breaker size, and defects inside the panel", p: "33–42",
  goal: "Match wire to breaker and recognize every common defect behind the dead front cover.",
  teach: [
    "# The wire must match the breaker",
    "- General rule for copper branch circuits: #14 wire = 15 amps, #12 = 20 amps, #10 = 30 amps. A smaller number is a thicker wire.",
    "- A breaker larger than the wire allows is the common defect. The wire can overheat before the breaker trips.",
    "- Exception: air conditioner and heat pump condensers. Read the condenser label. It gives the maximum breaker size and the minimum circuit ampacity. The breaker may look too big for the wire because motors pull a surge when they start. Check the breaker against the label; it is often not changed when a new condenser is installed.",
    "# How many wires under one screw",
    "- Hot and neutral wires: one wire per lug.",
    "- Equipment grounding wires: two per lug, if they are the same size.",
    "- Two wires on one breaker is called a double tap. The label can allow it; some Square D (Homeline) 15, 20 and 30 amp breakers are made for two wires.",
    "# Defects to report inside the enclosure",
    "- Rust (a sign of water) and contaminants such as paint or drywall compound: both raise resistance.",
    "- Nicked, damaged or missing strands; melted insulation or charred wire (a loose connection or overcurrent).",
    "- Bare conductor showing past the breaker case; wires bent sharply into lugs.",
    "- Pointed screws holding the cover: they can pierce insulation and energize the box.",
    "- Circuits not labeled. Labels must be permanent and specific.",
    "- A white wire used as a hot wire and not marked black or red.",
    "- Breaker brand different from the panel brand: recommend an electrician. Deciding if it is allowed is out of scope.",
    "- Missing or homemade handle ties on 240 volt and multiwire circuits.",
    "- Open knockouts or twist-out tabs: a shock hazard. Tape or cardboard is not a repair.",
    "- Low voltage parts (doorbell transformer, phone or coax cable) inside the enclosure.",
    "- More breakers than the label allows; half-height breakers in the wrong slots.",
    "- Panel rated higher than its supply wires.",
    "- Cables entering without a clamp.",
    "- A fused neutral (very old systems): if it opens, the circuit stays energized.",
    "! 14-15, 12-20, 10-30. For a condenser, read the label."
  ],
  cards: [
    ["Copper #14, #12, #10: maximum breaker", "15 amps, 20 amps, 30 amps."],
    ["Which circuit does not follow the 14-15, 12-20, 10-30 rule?", "Air conditioner and heat pump condensers. Use the maximum OPD and minimum circuit ampacity on the condenser label."],
    ["Wires per lug: hot and neutral", "One conductor per lug unless the label says otherwise."],
    ["Wires per lug: equipment grounding conductors", "Two, if they are the same size."],
    ["Melted insulation at a breaker means", "A loose connection or an overcurrent that was not interrupted."],
    ["White wire used as a hot", "Allowed, but it must be permanently re-marked (tape or marker) at all accessible points."],
    ["Open knockout in a panel", "A shock hazard. It must be closed with a proper filler, not tape or cardboard."],
    ["Why are pointed screws a defect on a panel cover?", "They can pierce wire insulation and energize the enclosure."]
  ],
  qs: [
    ["A #12 copper wire is connected to a 30 amp breaker serving bedroom receptacles. What is reported?", ["The breaker is too large for the wire: a fire hazard", "Nothing; 30 amps is correct for #12", "The wire is too large", "The breaker is too small"], "#12 copper is limited to 20 amps on a general branch circuit."],
    ["A condenser label reads: maximum breaker 30 amps, minimum circuit ampacity 18 amps. The circuit is #12 copper on a 30 amp breaker. Is this a defect?", ["No. Condenser circuits follow the label", "Yes. #12 wire may never be on a 30 amp breaker", "Yes. The breaker must be 15 amps", "Yes. The wire must be #6"], "The breaker is at the label maximum and the wire meets the minimum ampacity."],
    ["Two neutral wires are under one screw on the neutral bar. This is:", ["A defect: one neutral per lug", "Acceptable; neutrals may share", "Acceptable if both are white", "Required"], "Only EGCs may share a lug, two of the same size."],
    ["A breaker of a different brand than the panel is installed. What should the inspector do?", ["Recommend evaluation by an electrician", "State that it is definitely unsafe", "Ignore it if it fits", "Replace it"], "Some breakers are listed for other panels. Deciding which is out of scope, so recommend evaluation."],
    ["The inspector sees rust streaks at the bottom of the panel enclosure. What does this suggest?", ["Water is getting into the enclosure", "The panel is new", "Too many breakers", "Normal aging with no concern"], "Water increases resistance and can cause a fire. It often comes down the service entrance cable."]
  ] },

{ t: "Subpanels, detached buildings and problem panels", p: "43–48",
  goal: "Explain why neutrals float in a subpanel and name the panels to warn clients about.",
  teach: [
    "# Subpanels",
    "- For grounding, everything after the service equipment is a subpanel. That includes a main lug panel next to a separate main disconnect.",
    "- Rule: no connection between neutral and ground after the service disconnect.",
    "- Why: the neutral carries current. If neutrals touch the grounds or the metal box, that current also travels on bonding wires, metal boxes and appliance cases. If something goes wrong (reversed polarity, a fault), that metal can become live.",
    "- Correct subpanel: neutrals on a bar that is insulated from the box. Grounds on a separate bar that is bonded to the box.",
    "- Defect to report: neutrals and grounds on the same bar in a subpanel, or the neutral bar bonded to the enclosure.",
    "# Detached buildings",
    "- Fed by one 15 or 20 amp branch circuit with an EGC: no ground rod needed.",
    "- Fed by two or more circuits, or by a feeder to a subpanel: the building needs its own grounding electrode. New feeders have an EGC, connected to the electrode, with neutrals kept separate.",
    "- An old feeder with no EGC may stay. Then the panel is wired like a main panel: neutrals and grounds together, connected to the electrode and bonded to the box.",
    "# Problem panels",
    "- Federal Pacific Stab-Lok (FPE): mid-1950s to early 1980s. Breakers may not trip, may be live when they show off, and may arc at the bus. Breakers can fall out when the cover is removed; some inspectors do not open them. Only the Stab-Lok type is involved.",
    "- Zinsco / Zinsco-Sylvania: 1940s to 1970s. Same kinds of problems.",
    "- Bulldog Pushmatic: 1950s and 1960s mostly. Opinion is split; at least report age and that parts are hard to find.",
    "- What to do: report that the panel is present, explain the possible risk, and recommend evaluation by a qualified electrician. Most are also at the end of their service life; report that too.",
    "! Main: neutrals and grounds together. Sub: separate."
  ],
  cards: [
    ["Where may neutral and ground be connected together?", "Only at the service equipment (and at an old detached-building feeder with no EGC)."],
    ["Correct subpanel bars", "Neutral bar isolated from the enclosure; ground bar bonded to the enclosure."],
    ["Why separate neutrals and grounds in a subpanel?", "Neutral current would otherwise flow on EGCs and bonded metal, which could become energized."],
    ["Detached building fed by a feeder to a subpanel needs", "Its own grounding electrode."],
    ["Federal Pacific Stab-Lok", "Problem panel, mid-1950s to early 1980s: breakers may fail to trip. Report, explain risk, recommend an electrician."],
    ["Zinsco", "Problem panel, 1940s to 1970s: breakers may not trip and can arc at the bus."],
    ["What should be reported for a problem panel?", "Its presence, the potential risks, a recommendation for evaluation by a qualified electrician, and end of service life."]
  ],
  qs: [
    ["In a subpanel, the neutral bar is screwed directly to the metal enclosure with a green bonding screw. What is reported?", ["A defect: the neutral must be isolated from the enclosure", "Correct installation", "A cosmetic issue", "A missing handle tie"], "The bonding screw belongs only in service equipment."],
    ["A detached workshop is supplied by a feeder to its own panel. What should be present at the workshop?", ["A grounding electrode", "A second utility meter", "A 400 amp disconnect", "Nothing extra"], "Two or more circuits or a feeder require a grounding electrode at the detached building."],
    ["The inspector finds a Federal Pacific Stab-Lok panel. What is the best report?", ["Report its presence, explain the possible risks and recommend evaluation by a qualified electrician", "Say nothing if the lights work", "State that the house will burn", "Remove the breakers to test them"], "This is the majority practice. The inspector may also choose not to remove the cover."],
    ["A panel next to the meter has a separate main disconnect ahead of it in another box. How is this panel treated for grounding?", ["As a subpanel", "As service equipment", "As a meter base", "As a junction box"], "Any panel downstream of the service equipment is a subpanel."]
  ] },

{ t: "Wiring methods: cables", p: "48–55",
  goal: "Identify NM, UF, AC, MC and SE cable and know where each may be used.",
  teach: [
    "# Nonmetallic sheathed cable (NM)",
    "- The main wiring in most houses since the 1940s. Often called Romex, but that is a brand name; write NM or nonmetallic sheathed cable.",
    "- 14/2 means #14 wire with two insulated conductors, plus a ground.",
    "- Modern jacket colors: white = #14, yellow = #12, orange = #10, black = #8 and #6.",
    "- Dry indoor locations only. Not outdoors, not buried, not in concrete, and not in conduit that is outdoors or buried (those are wet locations).",
    "- Support every 4 1/2 feet and within 12 inches of a box (8 inches for most plastic boxes).",
    "- At least 1/4 inch of sheathing should enter the box.",
    "- In a basement, #10 and smaller NM goes through holes in the joists, not stapled to the bottom of them.",
    "# Underground feeder (UF)",
    "- Like NM but made for wet places and burial. Usually gray. Not for pools, hot tubs and spas, because its ground wire is bare.",
    "# Armored cable (AC) and metal-clad cable (MC)",
    "- AC is often called BX (a brand). Flexible spiral metal jacket. Conductors are wrapped in paper and it needs an anti-short bushing at the ends. The jacket can serve as the ground for AC made after 1959. Dry indoor locations only. Support every 4 1/2 feet.",
    "- MC looks similar (sometimes called Greenfield). No paper wrap. May be used in most places and buried if listed. Support every 6 feet.",
    "# Service entrance cable (SE)",
    "- Runs from the service point to the service equipment; also used for large appliances and feeders. Support every 30 inches. Not for burial (USE cable is).",
    "# Nail guards",
    "- Any cable closer than 1 1/4 inches to the edge of a stud or joist needs a steel plate to stop nails and screws.",
    "# Subject to physical damage",
    "- Not defined, so judgment. Usually includes cable under kitchen sinks, within reach on finished walls, on unfinished basement and garage walls, and within 7 feet vertically and 6 feet horizontally of an attic access. Crawl spaces usually do not count.",
    "! NM colors: White 14, Yellow 12, Orange 10, Black 8 and 6."
  ],
  cards: [
    ["NM cable jacket colors", "White = #14, yellow = #12, orange = #10, black = #8 and #6."],
    ["Where may NM cable be used?", "Interior dry locations only. Not outdoors, buried or in concrete, even inside conduit."],
    ["NM cable support", "Every 4 1/2 feet and within 12 inches of a box (8 inches for most plastic boxes)."],
    ["UF cable", "Like NM but for wet locations and burial; usually gray. Not for pools and spas."],
    ["AC (armored) cable", "Spiral metal jacket, paper-wrapped conductors, anti-short bushing, dry interior locations only. Often called BX."],
    ["When is a nail guard needed?", "When a cable or flexible tubing is closer than 1 1/4 inches to the edge of a framing member."],
    ["What does 12/2 NM mean?", "#12 conductors, two insulated conductors, plus an equipment grounding conductor."],
    ["Attic zone considered subject to physical damage", "Within 7 feet vertically and 6 feet horizontally of the attic access opening."]
  ],
  qs: [
    ["Yellow-jacketed NM cable is connected to a 30 amp breaker. What is the concern?", ["Yellow is #12, which is limited to 20 amps", "Yellow is #10, so it is correct", "Yellow is #14 and needs 15 amps", "Jacket color has no meaning"], "White 14, yellow 12, orange 10."],
    ["NM cable runs along the outside wall of the house to a shed, inside plastic conduit. Is this acceptable?", ["No. Outdoors is a wet location, even inside conduit", "Yes, conduit makes it acceptable", "Yes, if it is yellow", "Yes, if it is stapled"], "NM is for dry interior locations. UF or individual wet-rated conductors are used outdoors."],
    ["NM cable passes through a hole 3/4 inch from the face of a stud with no steel plate. What is reported?", ["Missing nail guard", "Nothing", "Cable too small", "Missing bushing"], "Protection is needed when the cable is closer than 1 1/4 inches to the edge."],
    ["Which cable is suitable for direct burial to a yard light?", ["UF", "NM", "AC", "SE"], "UF is made for wet locations and burial."],
    ["In a report, which name is best for the common plastic-sheathed house cable?", ["Nonmetallic sheathed cable (NM)", "Romex", "BX", "Greenfield"], "Brand names should be avoided in reports."]
  ] },

{ t: "Conduit, burial, cords and wiring defects", p: "49–59",
  goal: "Know the conduit types, burial depths, the rules for cords, and the wiring defects to report.",
  teach: [
    "# Conduit and tubing (raceways)",
    "- EMT (electrical metallic tubing, thin wall): steel or aluminum. Support every 10 feet. Can serve as the ground with proper fittings.",
    "- RMC and IMC (rigid and intermediate metal conduit): heavy pipe, allowed in all locations. Support every 10 feet.",
    "- PVC (rigid nonmetallic conduit): usually gray. Schedule 80 where it can be damaged. Not the same as PVC plumbing pipe; one may not replace the other. Support every 3 feet for 1 inch and smaller.",
    "- FMC (flexible metal conduit): spiral metal, interior use, not buried. Support every 4 1/2 feet.",
    "- LFMC / LFC (liquidtight flexible): flexible with a waterproof jacket. You see it at outdoor condensers, pools and hot tubs.",
    "- ENT (electrical nonmetallic tubing): flexible corrugated plastic. Support every 3 feet. Not buried.",
    "# Burial depths",
    "- UF and MC cable: 24 inches.",
    "- PVC and liquidtight conduit: 18 inches.",
    "- Rigid and intermediate metal conduit: 6 inches.",
    "- A GFCI protected residential branch circuit of 20 amps or less: 12 inches with any method.",
    "- Depth usually cannot be seen and is usually out of scope.",
    "# Flexible cords and extension cords",
    "- A cord is not permanent wiring. It may not be spliced or extended, run through or inside walls, ceilings or floors (including drop ceilings), or be made from NM cable. NM may not end in a plug.",
    "- Maximum cord lengths for kitchen appliances: disposer 36 inches; range hood and trash compactor 48 inches.",
    "# Report these wiring defects",
    "- Splices not inside a covered box; boxes without covers; open knockouts in boxes.",
    "- Cable entering a box with no clamp or bushing, or with the sheathing stripped back before the box.",
    "- Cable not secured near the box or along the run.",
    "- Indoor wiring methods used outdoors.",
    "- Repairs made with electrical tape.",
    "- A plug feeding permanent wiring; cords run through walls, floors or ceilings.",
    "! Burial: 24 cable, 18 plastic, 6 rigid metal, 12 if GFCI and 20 amps or less."
  ],
  cards: [
    ["Burial depth for UF cable", "24 inches (12 inches if a GFCI protected residential circuit of 20 amps or less)."],
    ["Burial depth for PVC conduit", "18 inches."],
    ["Burial depth for rigid metal conduit", "6 inches."],
    ["EMT support spacing", "Every 10 feet."],
    ["Where is liquidtight flexible conduit commonly seen?", "At outdoor HVAC equipment, swimming pools and hot tubs."],
    ["Three things a flexible cord may not do", "Be spliced or extended; pass through or inside walls, ceilings or floors; substitute for permanent wiring."],
    ["Maximum cord length for a food-waste disposer", "36 inches."],
    ["Can PVC plumbing pipe be used as electrical conduit?", "No. They are different products and may not be substituted for each other."]
  ],
  qs: [
    ["An extension cord runs through a hole in the garage ceiling to power an opener. What is reported?", ["A defect: cords may not pass through ceilings or replace permanent wiring", "Nothing", "A missing GFCI only", "A burial depth defect"], "Recommend that a receptacle be installed by an electrician."],
    ["White PVC plumbing pipe is used as conduit for wires to a detached garage. Is this acceptable?", ["No. Plumbing pipe may not be substituted for electrical conduit", "Yes", "Yes, if glued", "Yes, if painted gray"], "Electrical PVC is listed for the purpose; plumbing pipe is not."],
    ["Two cables are joined with wire connectors in an attic with no box. What is this defect?", ["Splice not contained in an approved, covered box", "Missing nail guard", "Double tap", "Overfusing"], "All splices and taps belong in a covered box (knob and tube taps are the historic exception)."],
    ["What is the usual minimum burial depth for UF cable not protected by a GFCI?", ["24 inches", "6 inches", "12 inches", "36 inches"], "24 for UF and MC, 18 for PVC, 6 for rigid metal conduit."],
    ["A length of NM cable has a plug attached and is used as an appliance cord. Is this acceptable?", ["No. NM may not end in a plug or be used as a flexible cord", "Yes", "Yes, for appliances under 15 amps", "Yes, in basements"], "Permanent wiring methods are not cords."]
  ] },

{ t: "Old wiring: knob and tube, cloth cable, aluminum", p: "61–66",
  goal: "Recognize old wiring types, explain their risks and say what to recommend.",
  teach: [
    "# Knob and tube",
    "- Early 1900s to the 1930s. Single wires held on ceramic knobs and passed through ceramic tubes in the framing.",
    "- Installed correctly: wires at least 1 inch from surfaces, run on the sides of joists (not on top), splices soldered and taped. Its own taps may be outside a box. A connection to a different wiring method must be inside a covered box.",
    "- Problems: it is at the end of its service life, it usually has no ground wire, the circuits are 15 amps and often overloaded, and the insulation may contain asbestos.",
    "- Thermal insulation over it is a reportable deficiency in most areas. The book says the real reasons are damage from attic traffic and wires pushed against framing, not ordinary overheating.",
    "- Report its presence (active or not) and recommend evaluation by an electrician with knob and tube experience. Advise the client to ask their insurance company and lender; some will not accept it. It may look dead where you can see it and still be live inside walls.",
    "# Cloth-covered NM (rag wire)",
    "- Late 1920s to about 1960, most common in the 1940s and 1950s. The cloth and the insulation get brittle; vermin chew it; usually no ground wire.",
    "# Solid aluminum branch wiring",
    "- Made from about 1964, in sizes #12 to #8.",
    "- Why it fails: aluminum expands more than copper when hot, so connections work loose. Over many cycles the wire deforms and gets thinner (creep, also called cold flow). It also oxidizes, and it corrodes where it touches other metals. All of these raise resistance, and resistance makes heat.",
    "- A better alloy came in 1972 and was required by 1981, but by about 1980 it was no longer sold.",
    "- Devices marked CU/AL did not solve it. Devices marked CO/ALR work and have been required since 1981.",
    "- Repairs: rewire with copper (best); COPALUM crimp connectors (recommended by the CPSC, costly, trained electricians only); AlumiConn connectors (cheaper). Changing only the switches and receptacles to CO/ALR does not fix creep in pre-1972 wire and does not cover lights.",
    "# Aluminum that is fine",
    "- Stranded aluminum, #8 and larger, for service wires, feeders and large appliances. Antioxidant paste is recommended, not required by the NEC.",
    "- Copper-clad aluminum (1970s): no known problems; rated as aluminum.",
    "- Tin-coated copper (1940s and 1950s): looks like aluminum. Look at the cut end for copper; rubber insulation, not plastic. It is near the end of its life.",
    "! Aluminum trouble = solid, small (#12 to #8), 1964 to about 1980."
  ],
  cards: [
    ["Years knob and tube was most popular", "Early 1900s to the 1930s."],
    ["Recommendation for active knob and tube", "Report it and recommend evaluation by an electrician experienced with it; advise checking with the insurer and lender."],
    ["Solid aluminum branch wiring years", "Began about 1964; gone by about 1980."],
    ["Creep (cold flow)", "After repeated heating and cooling, aluminum wire at a connection deforms and becomes smaller and brittle, raising resistance and heat."],
    ["Device marking that works with aluminum wire", "CO/ALR (required since 1981). CU/AL devices did not work well."],
    ["Repair recommended by the CPSC for aluminum wiring", "COPALUM crimp connectors (besides full rewiring with copper)."],
    ["Is stranded aluminum for service and feeder wires a problem?", "No. It is normal when used with properly rated terminals; antioxidant paste is recommended."],
    ["How to tell tin-coated copper from aluminum", "Copper shows at the cut end, and the insulation is rubber, not plastic. Common in the 1940s and 1950s."]
  ],
  qs: [
    ["Silver-colored solid #12 branch wires are seen in the panel of a 1968 house. What is the best report?", ["Solid aluminum branch wiring is present; explain the fire risk at connections and recommend evaluation by a qualified electrician", "No concern; aluminum is normal for all wiring", "Tighten every connection during the inspection", "Report it as tin-coated copper"], "1964 to about 1980 with small solid conductors is the problem period."],
    ["Why do connections on old solid aluminum wire overheat?", ["Expansion, creep and oxidation loosen the connection and raise resistance", "Aluminum carries too much voltage", "Aluminum cannot carry current", "The breakers are too small"], "More resistance means more heat (Joule's law)."],
    ["Large stranded aluminum conductors feed the range and the main panel. What should be reported about the material?", ["Nothing as a defect; this use is normal", "A fire hazard needing rewiring", "Knob and tube wiring", "A bootleg ground"], "Only the small solid aluminum branch wiring has the bad history."],
    ["Knob and tube wiring appears abandoned in the basement. What should the report say?", ["It is present and may still be active in hidden areas; recommend evaluation", "It is certainly dead", "Nothing", "It meets current standards"], "It can be inactive where visible and active inside walls."],
    ["Which statement about replacing only the receptacles and switches with CO/ALR devices in a 1966 aluminum-wired house is correct?", ["It does not fix creep in the old alloy and does not address lights", "It is the complete repair recommended by the CPSC", "It turns the wire into copper", "It is not allowed"], "It may help post-1972 wire, but the old alloy and other terminations remain a concern."]
  ] },

{ t: "Receptacles", p: "66–74",
  goal: "Know receptacle wiring, the spacing rules and the defects to report.",
  teach: [
    "# How a 120 volt receptacle is laid out",
    "- With the ground slot down: neutral is the larger slot on the left, hot is the smaller slot on the right, ground is at the bottom.",
    "- A duplex receptacle counts as two. Breaking off the tab lets the halves be on two circuits, or one half switched.",
    "- A 20 amp receptacle has a small sideways notch on the neutral slot. A 15 amp circuit takes 15 amp receptacles; a 20 amp circuit takes 15 or 20.",
    "- New receptacles should be 3-slot, grounded and tamper-resistant. Old ones may stay.",
    "- Heights are custom, not rules: about 12 inches at walls, about 45 inches at counters. Ground slot up or down is also only custom.",
    "# Ungrounded 3-slot receptacles",
    "- A common defect. Two approved fixes: a GFCI labeled NO EQUIPMENT GROUND, or run a ground wire.",
    "- A jumper from the ground screw to the neutral screw is a bootleg ground. A 3-light tester shows it as correct, but it is a shock hazard.",
    "# Spacing in newer houses",
    "- Rooms: no point along a wall more than 6 feet from a receptacle (so about every 12 feet). Any wall 2 feet wide or more counts. A floor receptacle counts if within 18 inches of the wall. A required receptacle is no higher than 66 inches.",
    "- Kitchen counters: no point more than 2 feet from a receptacle (about every 4 feet), GFCI. Not more than 20 inches above the counter and not face up.",
    "- Island or peninsula more than 2 feet by 1 foot: at least one. It may be on the cabinet side, no more than 12 inches below the top.",
    "- Bathroom: one GFCI within 3 feet of the outside edge of each sink. None in or above a tub or shower.",
    "- Outdoors: one at the front and one at the rear, within 78 inches of the ground, GFCI. One more at a deck, balcony or porch reached from inside.",
    "- Hallway 10 feet or longer: one. Laundry: one, GFCI. Unfinished basement: one, GFCI. Garage: one per car space, GFCI (new rule; older houses are not defective).",
    "- HVAC equipment: a receptacle within 25 feet and on the same level.",
    "# Covers",
    "- Wet location (rain can hit it): a cover that is weatherproof with a plug in it (bubble cover). Damp location (under a roof): weatherproof when closed.",
    "# Load on a general circuit",
    "- A fixed appliance: no more than 50% of the circuit rating. A plug-in appliance: no more than 80% (12 amps on a 15 amp circuit).",
    "# Report these",
    "- Loose, damaged, scorched, worn or painted receptacles; missing or loose cover plates.",
    "- Reverse polarity, not grounded, open neutral, no power.",
    "- Missing where required; installed where not allowed (above a tub).",
    "! Rooms 6 and 12. Counters 2 and 4. Bathroom sink 3 feet."
  ],
  cards: [
    ["Slots of a 120 volt receptacle, ground down", "Neutral: larger slot, left. Hot: smaller slot, right. Ground: bottom."],
    ["Room receptacle spacing", "No point along the wall more than 6 feet from a receptacle (about every 12 feet). Walls 2 feet or wider count."],
    ["Kitchen counter receptacle spacing", "No point more than 2 feet from a receptacle (about every 4 feet); GFCI; not more than 20 inches above the counter."],
    ["Bathroom receptacle location", "GFCI within 3 feet of the outside edge of each sink; none in or above a tub or shower."],
    ["Bootleg ground", "A jumper between the ground and neutral terminals of a receptacle. Tests as grounded but is a shock hazard."],
    ["Outdoor receptacles required", "One at the front and one at the rear, within 78 inches of grade, GFCI; plus one at a deck, balcony or porch accessible from inside."],
    ["Cover for a receptacle in a wet location", "Weatherproof while a plug is inserted (bubble cover)."],
    ["Largest plug-in appliance on a 15 amp general circuit", "12 amps (80%). A fixed appliance: 7.5 amps (50%)."]
  ],
  qs: [
    ["A 3-light tester shows correct wiring on 3-slot receptacles in a 1955 house with 2-wire cable. What should the inspector suspect?", ["Bootleg grounds", "Perfect wiring", "AFCI protection", "Aluminum wiring"], "With no ground wire in the cable, a correct reading suggests a neutral-to-ground jumper. Report and recommend evaluation."],
    ["On a kitchen counter the receptacles are 6 feet apart. Does this meet the modern spacing rule?", ["No. No point may be more than 2 feet from a receptacle, so about 4 feet apart", "Yes. 6 feet applies to counters", "Yes. 12 feet applies to counters", "Counters need no receptacles"], "6 and 12 are for room walls. 2 and 4 are for counters."],
    ["A receptacle is mounted on the wall inside a shower enclosure. This is:", ["Not allowed", "Allowed with a GFCI", "Allowed with a bubble cover", "Required"], "Receptacles may not be installed in or above tubs and showers."],
    ["An exterior receptacle on an open wall, exposed to rain, has a flat snap cover that only closes when nothing is plugged in. What is reported?", ["Wrong cover for a wet location", "Nothing", "Missing AFCI", "Reverse polarity"], "Wet locations need an in-use (bubble) cover."],
    ["A receptacle moves in the wall when a plug is pushed in. Why does this matter?", ["Wires can loosen, arc and overheat", "It is only cosmetic", "It lowers the voltage", "It trips the GFCI"], "Loose receptacles are a common, reportable defect."]
  ] },

{ t: "Switches, disconnects, lights and fans", p: "76–85",
  goal: "Know the required lights, closet and tub clearances, and how fans and disconnects are checked.",
  teach: [
    "# Switches",
    "- Maximum height 79 inches. No minimum. About 48 inches is custom. ON should be up (not for 3-way and 4-way switches).",
    "- A light controlled from two places uses two 3-way switches. From three or more places: 3-way at each end and 4-way switches between. Test them from every position.",
    "- Silent toggle switches from the 1960s and 1970s contain mercury (hazardous waste).",
    "- Low-voltage switching systems (1960s and 1970s) use relays; tell the client they need special parts.",
    "- No switches inside tubs and showers. Timers: use the manual override; testing the timer is out of scope.",
    "- Report: loose, damaged, warm, noisy (arcing) or painted switches; missing covers; 3-way switches that do not work in every position.",
    "# Appliance disconnects",
    "- Fixed appliances (furnace, air handler, condenser, baseboard heat, sump and ejector pumps, pool pump) need a way to shut off power for service.",
    "- The breaker counts if the panel is within sight of the appliance. If not, a separate disconnect is needed near the appliance. A plug counts for cord-connected appliances such as a disposer or dishwasher.",
    "- Look inside disconnect boxes that have fuse blocks or pull-outs.",
    "# Required lights",
    "- A wall-switched light in every habitable room and bathroom. In kitchens and bathrooms it must be a fixed light; in other rooms a switched receptacle is allowed.",
    "- A switched fixed light in hallways, stairways, garages and at exterior doors (not the vehicle door).",
    "- Stairs with six or more risers: a switch at the top and at the bottom.",
    "- Attics, crawl spaces and basements used for storage or holding equipment: at least one light, with the switch at the entrance.",
    "- Low voltage lighting is out of scope.",
    "# Fixture support",
    "- Up to 6 pounds: an ordinary box. Over 6 pounds: a box listed for it. Over 50 pounds: supported independently of the box.",
    "# Near tubs and showers",
    "- No hanging fixtures, track lights or ceiling fans within 3 feet horizontally and 8 feet vertically of the tub rim or shower threshold. Fixtures over a tub or shower must be listed for damp or wet locations.",
    "# Clothes closets",
    "- No exposed incandescent bulbs anywhere in a clothes closet.",
    "- Surface incandescent or LED fixtures: 12 inches from the storage space. Surface fluorescent and all recessed fixtures: 6 inches.",
    "# Ceiling fans and recessed lights",
    "- A ceiling fan is an appliance. It needs a box labeled for fan support. A wobbling fan is a defect. If it only works by a remote you cannot find, report that it was not operated.",
    "- Recessed lights that are not IC rated need 3 inches from insulation. Unsealed cans leak air and can add to ice dams and attic moisture.",
    "! Closet: 12 for surface bulbs and LED, 6 for fluorescent and recessed, bare bulbs never."
  ],
  cards: [
    ["Maximum switch or breaker handle height", "79 inches above the floor."],
    ["When is a separate appliance disconnect needed?", "When the panel is not within sight of the fixed appliance."],
    ["Stairway switch rule", "Six or more risers: a switch at both the top and the bottom."],
    ["Light fixture weight limits", "Up to 6 pounds on a normal box; over 6 pounds needs a listed box; over 50 pounds needs independent support."],
    ["Zone over a tub with no hanging fixtures or fans", "3 feet horizontally and 8 feet vertically from the tub rim or shower threshold."],
    ["Closet clearance: surface incandescent or LED fixture", "12 inches from the storage area."],
    ["Closet clearance: fluorescent or recessed fixture", "6 inches from the storage area."],
    ["Non-IC recessed light clearance to insulation", "At least 3 inches."],
    ["Box for a ceiling fan", "Must be labeled as suitable for ceiling fan support."]
  ],
  qs: [
    ["A bare bulb in a porcelain socket is on the ceiling of a bedroom clothes closet. What is reported?", ["A fire hazard: exposed incandescent bulbs are not allowed in clothes closets", "Acceptable if 6 inches from the shelf", "Acceptable if 12 inches from the shelf", "Acceptable with a pull chain"], "Exposed or partly exposed incandescent bulbs are not allowed anywhere in a clothes closet."],
    ["The air conditioner condenser is outside and the panel is in the basement. What should be at the condenser?", ["A disconnect within sight of the unit", "A GFCI receptacle only", "A second meter", "Nothing"], "The breaker serves as the disconnect only when the panel is within sight."],
    ["A ceiling fan hangs directly over a bathtub, 7 feet above the rim. Is this acceptable?", ["No. No part may be within 8 feet vertically and 3 feet horizontally of the tub rim", "Yes", "Yes, if it has a light", "Yes, if it is plastic"], "The zone applies to hanging fixtures, track lights and ceiling fans."],
    ["A stairway has 10 risers and a single switch at the bottom only. What is reported?", ["A switch is needed at both top and bottom", "Nothing", "A missing GFCI", "The light must be fluorescent"], "Six or more risers require control from both levels (3-way switches)."],
    ["A ceiling fan can only be run by a remote control that cannot be found. What should the inspector do?", ["Report that the fan was not operated and why", "Report the fan as working", "Wire it to a wall switch", "Leave it out of the report"], "Anything required that is not inspected or operated is reported with the reason."]
  ] },

{ t: "Smoke and carbon monoxide alarms; systems that are out of scope", p: "85–92",
  goal: "Know where alarms go, how long they last, and what to say about generators and solar.",
  teach: [
    "# What the standard asks",
    "- Usually only to report whether smoke alarms and carbon monoxide (CO) alarms are present or absent. Testing is usually not required.",
    "- Pressing the test button may only test the sounder, not the sensor. If you test, tell the client this. Never use a smoke pen or other unlisted smoke to test.",
    "# Two kinds of smoke alarm",
    "- Ionization: the most common. A little better at fast flaming fires, much slower at smoky smoldering fires, and more false alarms.",
    "- Photoelectric: good at smoky, smoldering fires, which is how many house fires begin.",
    "- You usually cannot tell the type from below.",
    "# Service life",
    "- Smoke alarms: replace after about 10 years. CO alarms: about 5 to 7 years. A combination unit can lose its CO part first.",
    "# Where smoke alarms go",
    "- In each bedroom, in the hallway near the bedrooms, and on each story including basements and habitable attics.",
    "- Not in crawl spaces or uninhabitable attics.",
    "- On the ceiling at least 4 inches from the wall, or on the wall with the top 4 to 12 inches below the ceiling.",
    "- Not in kitchens or garages, not near furnaces, water heaters or fireplaces (about 20 feet away is suggested), not in bathrooms or laundry rooms, not near fans, windows that open or fluorescent lights.",
    "- New houses: all alarms interconnected, powered by house wiring, with battery backup.",
    "# CO alarms",
    "- Required in new houses that have a fuel-burning appliance (a fireplace counts) or an attached garage. Located near the bedrooms.",
    "- The 12 inch ceiling rule does not apply; lower on the wall is fine, above the reach of children.",
    "# Out of scope: report, disclaim, recommend",
    "- Load controllers: utility devices that can switch off a condenser at peak times. Mention it and suggest the client call the utility.",
    "- Generators: portable about 3,000 to 10,000 watts; permanent about 7,000 to 22,000 watts or more. A transfer switch must disconnect the house from the grid; backfeeding can kill utility workers. Report the generator and transfer switch, disclaim them, and recommend a specialist.",
    "- Alternative energy (solar PV, wind, solar water heating): solar panels make DC power. Report that the system is present, disclaim it, recommend a specialist, and suggest the client ask for the permit and inspection records.",
    "! Smoke 10 years, CO 5 to 7. Ceiling 4 inches from the wall; wall 4 to 12 inches down."
  ],
  cards: [
    ["Smoke alarm service life", "About 10 years."],
    ["Carbon monoxide alarm service life", "About 5 to 7 years."],
    ["Ionization vs photoelectric", "Ionization: slightly better at flaming fires, slow on smoky fires, more false alarms. Photoelectric: good at smoldering, smoky fires."],
    ["Required smoke alarm locations", "Each bedroom, the hallway near bedrooms, and each story including basements and habitable attics."],
    ["Smoke alarm placement on a wall", "Top of the alarm 4 to 12 inches below the ceiling. On a ceiling: at least 4 inches from the wall."],
    ["When is a CO alarm required (new construction)?", "When the house has a fuel-burning appliance, including a fireplace, or an attached garage."],
    ["Purpose of a generator transfer switch", "It disconnects the house from the utility grid so the generator cannot backfeed and shock utility workers."],
    ["How to report solar panels or a generator", "Report that it is present, disclaim inspection, and recommend evaluation by a specialist."]
  ],
  qs: [
    ["What do most standards of practice require about smoke alarms?", ["Report their presence or absence", "Test each sensor with smoke", "Replace old alarms", "Measure the sound level"], "Testing is the exception, and the test button may not test the sensor."],
    ["A smoke alarm is mounted on a wall with its top 20 inches below the ceiling. Is this correct?", ["No. It should be 4 to 12 inches below the ceiling", "Yes", "No. It should be on the floor", "No. It must touch the ceiling corner"], "Smoke collects near the ceiling, but the corner within 4 inches is dead air."],
    ["Which smoke alarm type responds better to a slow, smoky fire?", ["Photoelectric", "Ionization", "Both are the same", "Neither"], "Ionization alarms may respond too late to smoldering fires."],
    ["A portable generator is wired into the panel with no transfer switch. What is the main hazard?", ["Backfeeding the utility lines and shocking utility workers", "Too little voltage", "The lights will flicker", "Fuel use"], "The house must be disconnected from the grid whenever the generator feeds it."],
    ["An all-electric house has no fuel-burning appliances but has an attached garage. Is a CO alarm called for in new construction?", ["Yes, because of the attached garage", "No, never", "Only in the garage itself", "Only if there is a pool"], "Car exhaust from an attached garage is a carbon monoxide source."],
    ["The roof has solar panels. What should the report say?", ["A photovoltaic system is present; it was not inspected; recommend a specialist", "The system produces the rated power", "Nothing", "The panels must be removed"], "Alternative energy systems are out of scope. Also suggest asking for permits."]
  ] },
{ t: "Solar, wind and solar water heating up close", p: "92–100",
  goal: "Recognize the parts of solar and wind systems, their service life, and the defects you can see.",
  teach: [
    "# The rule for all alternative energy",
    "- Solar PV, wind turbines, solar water heating, passive solar and ground- or water-source heat pumps are out of scope. Report that the system is there, disclaim it, recommend a specialist, and suggest asking for the permit and approved inspections.",
    "- If you choose to comment on them anyway, state the limits of what you did.",
    "# Solar photovoltaic (PV)",
    "- Cells make a small DC current at low voltage. Cells form modules, modules form panels, panels form arrays.",
    "- Grid-connected (also called grid-tied or utility-interactive): the most common. It helps power the house in daylight, the utility covers the rest, and extra power is fed back to the grid.",
    "- Stand-alone: not tied to the grid; batteries or a generator cover nights and cloudy days.",
    "- Path of the power: array → DC disconnect → inverter (turns DC into AC) → AC disconnect → a backfed breaker in the house panel. That breaker should be labeled and secured in place.",
    "- Conductors run in conduit (EMT or MC is common) except where they enter equipment or must move with the array. Roof and wall penetrations need waterproofing.",
    "- Service life: panels about 20 to 25 years (output drops with age), inverters about 5 to 15 years, other parts about 30 to 50 years.",
    "- Visible defects: system off or not working (the inverter display usually shows status), backfed breaker not secured, missing labels, missing disconnects, exposed conductors, metal parts not bonded, panels covering vents or exhaust openings, heavy shading of the array.",
    "# Wind turbines",
    "- A rotor turns a generator on a tower. The blades should be at least 30 feet above anything within 300 feet. About one acre of land is a suggested minimum.",
    "- A tower of 60 feet or more should be allowed by zoning; 100 feet or more is usually recommended. An average wind speed of at least 10 mph is a suggested minimum.",
    "- Needs a surge protective device (lightning arrestor) between the turbine and the house, and the tower must be connected to a grounding electrode.",
    "- Rotor and generator service life: about 5 to 15 years, like an inverter.",
    "- Defects: the same as PV, plus a tower that is not grounded and damaged or deteriorated parts.",
    "# Solar water heating",
    "- Most systems preheat water for a normal water heater.",
    "- Direct systems heat the house water itself in the collector and must be shut down and drained in freezing weather. Indirect systems heat a non-freezing transfer fluid that heats the water through a heat exchanger. Active systems use a pump.",
    "- Collector types: batch (a black tank in an insulated box), flat plate (pipes with black metal fins), insulated tube (glass tubes).",
    "- Toxic or flammable transfer fluids are not allowed.",
    "- Service life: collectors 20 years or more; pumps and controls about 5 to 15 years. Direct systems are prone to mineral buildup.",
    "- Defects: missing backflow preventer, missing or wrong relief valve or extension pipe, missing or damaged pipe insulation, leaks, corrosion, missing expansion tank, missing labels.",
    "! PV path: array, DC disconnect, inverter, AC disconnect, labeled backfed breaker. Panels 20 to 25 years, inverter 5 to 15."
  ],
  cards: [
    ["What an inverter does", "Turns the DC power from solar panels (or a wind generator) into AC power the house can use."],
    ["Grid-connected vs stand-alone PV", "Grid-connected works with the utility and can send extra power back. Stand-alone has no grid; batteries or a generator fill gaps."],
    ["Order of parts in a grid-tied PV system", "Array, DC disconnect, inverter, AC disconnect, backfed breaker in the panel."],
    ["Solar panel service life", "About 20 to 25 years, with output dropping as they age."],
    ["Inverter service life", "About 5 to 15 years."],
    ["Wind turbine blade clearance", "At least 30 feet above obstructions within 300 feet."],
    ["Direct vs indirect solar water heating", "Direct heats the house water in the collector and must be drained in freezing weather. Indirect heats a non-freezing fluid and uses a heat exchanger."],
    ["How a home inspector handles alternative energy", "Report it is present, disclaim it, recommend a specialist, and suggest asking for permits and inspections."]
  ],
  qs: [
    ["Solar panels feed a breaker in the main panel. The breaker is not held in place and has no label. What do you report?", ["The backfed breaker should be labeled and secured; recommend an electrician", "Nothing; solar is out of scope so nothing may be said", "Remove the breaker", "The panels are near end of life"], "Even out-of-scope systems can show visible defects worth reporting."],
    ["About how long do solar PV inverters usually last?", ["5 to 15 years", "1 to 2 years", "20 to 25 years", "50 years or more"], "Panels last longer, about 20 to 25 years."],
    ["A direct solar water heating system is in a cold climate. What must happen in freezing weather?", ["It must be shut down and drained", "Nothing", "The pump must run faster", "It should be switched to indirect"], "Direct systems hold house water in the collector, which can freeze."],
    ["What must be installed between a wind turbine and the loads in the house?", ["A surge protective device (lightning arrestor)", "A water heater", "A smoke alarm", "A GFCI on every outlet"], "The tower must also be connected to a grounding electrode."],
    ["Which item is a visible PV defect a home inspector may report?", ["Panels covering a plumbing vent opening", "The utility rate", "The panel brand", "The array angle"], "Covering vents or exhaust openings is a listed defect unless enough space is left."]
  ] },

{ t: "Details people miss: fuses, breakers, cables, receptacles, lights", p: "2, 30–31, 49–55, 70–81",
  goal: "Pick up the smaller rules from the chapter that are easy to overlook but show up on the exam.",
  teach: [
    "# Service types",
    "- Power is generated as three phase and changed to single phase for almost all houses. Three phase service (three energized conductors and one grounded conductor) is mostly commercial and is very uncommon in houses.",
    "# Fuses",
    "- Type W fuses open almost at once. They are fine on lighting circuits but wrong for motors, which draw a surge when they start.",
    "- Type L and Type D fuses have a short time delay and are usually better for general use.",
    "- Fuse color code: 30 amp green; 25 amp green or yellow; 20 amp orange or red; 15 amp blue or purple; 10 amp yellow or slate (can look gray). Labels can be faded, so look inside or elsewhere on the case.",
    "- Cartridge fuses (cylinder shaped) still turn up as service equipment in old breaker panels, in service switches and in HVAC disconnect boxes. 100 amp and larger cartridge fuses usually have blades on both ends.",
    "- A penny or similar object in a fuse holder: report it and recommend evaluation of the circuit.",
    "# Breaker case styles",
    "- Full-height (1 inch) and half-height (1/2 inch) breakers protect one 120 volt circuit. A tandem protects one 240 volt circuit, or two breakers joined by a handle tie do.",
    "- Some panels do not accept half-height breakers at all, even if they seem to fit, and some allow them only in certain slots. The panel label says which slots and the maximum number of breakers.",
    "# Cables",
    "- Bend radius: cables like AC and NM should not be bent tighter than five times the cable diameter.",
    "- AC, MC, FMC and ENT: support about every 4 1/2 feet and within 1 foot of boxes; nail guards when closer than 1 1/4 inches to the edge of a stud or joist.",
    "- UF may not be used for pool, hot tub or spa wiring because its equipment ground is bare.",
    "- Low voltage wiring (doorbell transformers, phone, coaxial cable) may not share an enclosure or conduit with house voltage wiring unless there is a non-conductive barrier.",
    "# Receptacles you might forget",
    "- A foyer larger than 60 square feet needs a receptacle in each wall space at least 3 feet wide.",
    "- A hallway 10 feet or longer needs at least one receptacle.",
    "- Each vehicle bay of an attached garage needs at least one GFCI protected receptacle.",
    "# Lights",
    "- In new homes at least 75% of light bulbs should be high-efficacy (LED, CFL, fluorescent).",
    "- House lighting circuits are 15 or 20 amps at 120 volts. Low voltage lights (usually 12 or 24 volts), such as landscape lights, are out of scope.",
    "- Halogen bulbs get very hot and need their own special socket.",
    "! Fuse colors: 30 green, 20 orange or red, 15 blue. Bend no tighter than 5 times the cable width."
  ],
  cards: [
    ["Type W fuse", "Opens almost at once; fine for lighting circuits, wrong for motors."],
    ["Type L and Type D fuses", "Short time delay before opening; usually better for general use and motors."],
    ["15 amp fuse color", "Blue or purple."],
    ["20 amp fuse color", "Orange or red."],
    ["30 amp fuse color", "Green."],
    ["Where cartridge fuses still appear", "As service equipment in old breaker panels, in service switches, and in HVAC disconnect boxes."],
    ["Minimum cable bend radius", "Five times the cable diameter."],
    ["Foyer receptacle rule", "Foyers over 60 square feet need a receptacle in each wall space at least 3 feet wide."],
    ["High-efficacy lighting rule (new homes)", "At least 75% of bulbs should be high-efficacy, such as LED or CFL."],
    ["Three phase service in houses", "Very uncommon; it is mostly for commercial and industrial buildings."]
  ],
  qs: [
    ["A 15 amp circuit with 14 gauge wire has a green fuse. What is likely wrong?", ["The fuse is probably 30 amp, too large for the wire", "Nothing; green means safe", "The fuse is too small", "The wire is aluminum"], "Green means 30 amp. Overfusing can let the wire overheat."],
    ["A Type W fuse protects a well pump motor and keeps blowing at start-up. Why?", ["Type W opens almost at once, so the motor's starting surge trips it", "The fuse is cartridge type", "The pump needs a smaller fuse", "Type W fuses are only for 240 volts"], "A time-delay fuse (Type L or D) fits motor circuits better."],
    ["NM cable is bent into a tight kink much sharper than its own width. What rule is broken?", ["Bend radius should be at least 5 times the cable diameter", "Cable must never bend", "Bends need a junction box", "Nothing"], "Tight bends can damage the conductors."],
    ["Half-height breakers are installed in every slot of a panel whose label allows them only in four slots. What do you report?", ["Breakers installed against the panel label; recommend an electrician", "Nothing, they fit", "The panel is undersized for the house", "Remove all breakers"], "The label sets where half-height breakers may go and how many breakers are allowed."],
    ["A new home's foyer is 80 square feet with a 5 foot wall space and no receptacle. What do you report?", ["Missing receptacle; foyers over 60 sq ft need one in each 3 ft wall space", "Nothing; foyers never need one", "A GFCI is needed", "Too many receptacles"], "This is one of the less known receptacle rules."],
    ["Doorbell transformer wires run inside the main panel touching the house wiring. What do you report?", ["Low voltage wiring mixed with house voltage wiring without a barrier", "Normal practice", "Doorbells are out of scope so nothing", "The doorbell is too loud"], "Low and high voltage must be separated by a non-conductive barrier."]
  ] }

]);
